from . import T
T('8-er-relations','er','ER-model','Entities, relationships and ER constraints',r'''
An entity is a distinguishable object in the modeled world; an entity set groups similar objects. Attributes describe entities, and a key identifies each entity. Relationships connect participating entities and can carry their own attributes, such as a grade belonging to an enrollment rather than a student alone. A weak entity needs an owner plus a partial key for identification.
Cardinality specifies how many entities can participate on a side: one-to-one, one-to-many or many-to-many. Participation specifies whether every entity must appear in the relationship, separate from its maximum cardinality. Read diagram conventions carefully because arrows and double lines vary by notation.
Map strong entities to relations; many-to-many relationships need a relation containing participating keys and relationship attributes. A one-to-many relationship can often use a foreign key on the many side, with nullability reflecting optional participation. Repeated events require an event identifier or additional term/attempt attributes; two entity keys are not automatically a unique relationship key. Derive constraints from the scenario rather than a sample diagram’s visual spacing.
''','Model students enrolling in courses, with one grade per student/course pair.',r'Entity tables::::Student(student_id,…) and Course(course_id,…) have their own keys.~Relationship table::::Enrollment(student_id,course_id,grade) stores the many-to-many relationship and its grade.~Key and references::::The identifier pair is the enrollment key under the one-enrollment assumption; each identifier also references its entity.', 'Three relations with grade attached to Enrollment.','If repeat attempts are allowed, the pair alone no longer uniquely identifies every enrollment event.')
T('8-er-relations','relations','Relational model;Keys;Integrity constraints','Relations, keys and integrity constraints',r'''
A relation has a schema of named attributes and a set of tuples under mathematical set semantics. A candidate key is a minimal attribute set functionally determining every attribute. A superkey also determines all attributes but may include unnecessary ones; a primary key is one chosen candidate key. Prime attributes belong to at least one candidate key, not merely the chosen primary key.
Domain constraints restrict values; entity integrity prevents null primary-key components; referential integrity requires nonnull foreign-key values to match permitted referenced keys. A foreign key need not be unique in the referencing table: many rows can name the same parent. SQL tables commonly permit duplicates unless constraints prevent them, so distinguish implementation bag behavior from mathematical relations.
Keys are constraints over every legal state, not accidental uniqueness in a small sample. A name appearing once today does not prove a schema key. Composite foreign keys should be checked as tuples with the specified null rules. Additional total participation and cardinality constraints may require uniqueness, nonnull declarations or richer checks rather than one foreign key alone.
''','Student_id uniquely identifies students. Classify {student_id} and {student_id,name}.',r'Determination::::Either set contains student_id, so both uniquely determine a legal row.~Minimality::::Removing name from the larger set still preserves identification.~Classification::::The singleton is a candidate key; the larger set is only a superkey unless another assumption changes minimality.', 'Candidate key {student_id}; nonminimal superkey {student_id,name}.','A key test needs both uniqueness and inability to remove an attribute while retaining it.')
T('8-queries','algebra','Relational algebra','Relational algebra: selection, projection and joins',r'''
Relational algebra builds relations through operations. Selection σ filters tuples by a predicate and preserves attributes. Projection π keeps specified attributes and removes duplicate resulting tuples under set semantics. Cartesian product forms every tuple pairing; a theta join filters that product by a condition. Natural join matches all shared attribute names and includes one copy of each shared attribute.
Union, intersection and difference require compatible schemas/domains. Rename resolves attribute ambiguity and enables a relation to join with itself. Division expresses an “associated with every required value” query by finding candidate values with no missing required partner.
Operator order matters: projecting away an attribute before a selection that needs it makes that predicate unavailable. A natural join can accidentally match an unrelated shared name, so use explicit conditions when names do not encode the intended relationship. SQL may retain duplicates, giving different cardinalities from set algebra. GATE query-equivalence problems require the stated semantics and constraints, not just similar-looking expressions.
''','R(A,B)={(1,x),(2,x),(3,y)}. Select B=x, then project B.',r'Select::\sigma_{B=x}(R)=\{(1,x),(2,x)\}::Two rows satisfy the predicate.~Project::\pi_B=\{x\}::Both rows yield the same one-attribute tuple.~Deduplicate::::Set projection contains x once, not twice.', 'Result {(x)} with one tuple.','SQL SELECT B without DISTINCT would normally preserve two x rows, demonstrating the set/bag distinction.')
T('8-queries','calculus','Tuple calculus','Tuple relational calculus and safe queries',r'''
Tuple relational calculus describes what tuples satisfy a logical condition rather than an operation sequence. A query has form {t | P(t)} with relation-membership predicates, attribute comparisons and quantifiers. Free tuple variables describe the output; bound variables express existence or universal requirements. This connects database querying to first-order logic.
A safe, domain-independent query draws results from values constrained by the database and permitted constants, so it remains finite and does not depend on an arbitrary infinite external domain. An unconstrained negation such as all tuples not in a finite relation can range over infinitely many possibilities and is unsafe. Negation becomes meaningful when candidates are bounded by a relation.
Existential predicates express matching joins; universal requirements can be rewritten as absence of a counterexample. Quantifier scope matters just as in logic: one common witness differs from a witness per candidate. For GATE, identify output variables, candidate domain and predicate meaning before translating to algebra or SQL. The calculus is declarative, but safety restrictions distinguish useful finite queries from unrestricted formulas.
''','Express students enrolled in every required course using a counterexample formulation.',r'Bound candidates::::Choose s from Student, not all possible arbitrary tuples.~Define missing requirement::::A required course c is a counterexample if no Enrollment links s to c.~Negate existence::::Return s only when no such required c exists.', 'Return bounded students with no missing required enrollment.','If Required is empty, every candidate student satisfies the universal condition vacuously.')
T('8-queries','sql','SQL;Grouping','SQL evaluation order, grouping and nulls',r'''
SQL query reasoning normally begins with FROM and joins, then WHERE row filtering, GROUP BY, HAVING group filtering and SELECT output, with DISTINCT/order/limits at their relevant stages. This is a logical model, not a guarantee of physical execution order. Aggregate predicates require the completed group and belong in HAVING rather than a row WHERE clause.
COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(column) ignores null column values. SUM/AVG generally ignore null operands, and empty-input aggregates have specified null behavior rather than always returning zero. Comparisons involving NULL normally yield UNKNOWN; WHERE keeps only TRUE rows, while IS NULL directly tests nullness.
SQL uses bags unless duplicate removal is requested. Grouping partitions surviving rows by the grouping keys, so join multiplication can alter aggregate results. A NOT IN subquery containing NULL can produce UNKNOWN for unmatched values, unlike an appropriately correlated NOT EXISTS. Trace actual rows through each stage before counting; memorizing clause names does not reveal null or duplicate effects.
''','Rows in department D have scores 7,NULL,9. Compute COUNT(*), COUNT(score), AVG(score).',r'Row count::COUNT(*)=3::All three rows exist.~Nonnull count::COUNT(score)=2::The null score is excluded.~Average::AVG(score)=(7+9)/2=8::Use the number of nonnull values as divisor.', '3, 2 and 8 respectively.','Dividing 16 by 3 would incorrectly treat the unknown score as a numeric zero contribution.')
T('8-queries','joins','Joins','Inner, outer and self joins with duplicate multiplicities',r'''
An inner join emits row pairs satisfying its condition. A left outer join additionally preserves unmatched left rows, supplying nulls for right-side attributes; right/full joins preserve other sides accordingly. A self join uses aliases to compare distinct roles drawn from one table, such as an employee and manager.
Multiplicity matters under SQL bags: two left rows and three right rows with one matching key produce six joined rows. Equality of join keys does not deduplicate records automatically. Keys and foreign keys can constrain multiplicity, but only when those constraints actually exist.
A WHERE predicate on a right-side attribute after a left join can discard null-extended rows and effectively undo outer preservation. Placing the predicate in ON filters matches while still allowing unmatched left rows. Natural joins infer conditions from all shared column names, which can accidentally constrain more than intended. In GATE cardinality questions, count matching pairs per key and add unmatched preserved rows using the chosen join’s rules.
''','L has keys [1,1,2], R has [1,1,1,3]. Count inner and left join rows on key.',r'Matching key one::2\cdot3=6::Every qualifying left/right pair is a row.~Other keys::::Left key 2 has no match; right key 3 does not contribute to an inner or left join.~Preserve left::::The left join adds one null-extended row for key 2.', 'Inner join 6 rows; left join 7 rows.','The repeated key values remain multiple source rows; counting distinct keys would give the wrong result.')
T('8-normalization','fds','Functional dependencies;Closure','Functional dependencies and attribute closure',r'''
A functional dependency X→Y states that every pair of legal relation tuples agreeing on X also agrees on Y. It is a schema constraint, not an assertion that X values are unique unless Y includes all attributes. Dependencies can be inferred using reflexivity, augmentation and transitivity, and their consequences matter even if not listed explicitly.
Closure X⁺ starts with X and repeatedly adds an FD’s right side when its entire left side is already included. Stop at a fixed point. X is a superkey when closure includes the full schema, and a candidate key when no proper subset is a superkey. A multiattribute determinant requires all its members before applying its FD.
Dependencies inferred from one sample can fail in another legal state; given constraints govern the analysis. To compare FD sets, test whether each given dependency follows from the other set using closures. Minimal covers reduce redundant attributes/dependencies while preserving implication. Normalization and decomposition proofs require full inferred behavior, not simply checking visually present arrows.
''','R(A,B,C,D), FDs A→B,A→C,BC→D. Find A⁺.',r'Start::A^+=\{A\}::Every attribute set determines itself.~Use A dependencies::A^+=\{A,B,C\}::Both right sides become available.~Use joint determinant::BC\to D\Rightarrow A^+=\{A,B,C,D\}::B and C are now both present.', 'A is a superkey and, as a singleton, a candidate key assuming the empty set is not a key.','Starting from B alone cannot use BC→D until C is also known.')
T('8-normalization','normalforms','Normal forms;2NF;3NF;BCNF','2NF, 3NF and BCNF test different dependency problems',r'''
First normal form uses the relation model’s atomic attribute values. Second normal form prevents a nonprime attribute from depending on a proper subset of a candidate key. This requires finding all candidate keys; a single-attribute key has no nonempty proper subset, but another composite key still matters. Prime means belonging to some candidate key.
Third normal form permits every nontrivial X→A only when X is a superkey or A is prime. BCNF removes the prime-right-side exception and requires every nontrivial determinant to be a superkey. These are definitions over all implied dependencies. A dependency chain causing a nonprime value to depend on a nonkey determinant is a common 3NF violation, but apply the precise rule rather than a slogan about “transitivity.”
Normalization separates repeated facts to avoid update, insert and delete anomalies. Decomposition still needs independent checks for lossless join and dependency preservation. A BCNF decomposition can lose preservation even when lossless, while 3NF synthesis can preserve dependencies under its algorithm. Do not rank a proposed schema solely by whether it has a chosen primary key.
''','R(A,B,C) has A→B,B→C. Determine its highest normal form under ordinary atomic attributes.',r'Keys::::A⁺ is ABC and no FD derives A; A is the sole candidate key.~2NF::::The singleton key has no nonempty proper part, so no partial nonprime dependency violates 2NF.~3NF and BCNF::::B is not a superkey and C is nonprime; B→C violates both.', 'The relation is in 2NF but not 3NF or BCNF.','Checking only A→B would miss the nonkey determinant B and incorrectly report a stronger normal form.')
T('8-normalization','decomposition','Lossless decomposition;Dependency preservation','Losslessness and dependency preservation are separate proofs',r'''
A decomposition projects one relation into component schemas. Lossless join means joining those projections reconstructs exactly every original relation satisfying the dependencies, with no spurious extra tuples. For a binary split, the intersection must functionally determine all attributes of at least one component. A shared column alone is insufficient.
Dependency preservation means the original constraints can be enforced using the projected dependencies within components, without joining them. Projections include dependencies implied by the original FD set, not just originally printed arrows that happen to fit. A decomposition can be lossless but not preserving, or fail losslessness despite looking more normalized.
When testing, write each component’s attributes, their intersection and the closures proving the binary criterion. Then compute the union of locally enforceable dependencies and test whether it implies every original FD. A join dependency question differs from asking whether a particular sample happens to rejoin correctly; properties must hold for every legal state. Additional new topic labels here point to explicit proofs rather than only a final split recommendation.
''','Split R(A,B,C) with A→B,B→C into AB and BC.',r'Intersection::AB\cap BC=\{B\}::This is the shared attribute set.~Losslessness::B\to BC::The intersection determines the whole second relation.~Preservation::::A→B is local to AB and B→C local to BC; both can be checked without a join.', 'The decomposition is lossless and dependency-preserving.','The proofs establish two distinct properties; success on one did not automatically establish the other.')
T('8-storage-index','files','File organization;Indexing','File organizations, dense/sparse and clustered indexes',r'''
Heap file organization places records where space is available without a key order; sorted organization arranges them by a search key and favors ordered scans. An index stores search keys with pointers to records or data regions, adding maintenance and storage to accelerate selected accesses. Hash indexing supports equality well but not general ordered ranges.
A dense index has an entry for each search-key value or record under its convention; a sparse index has selected entries, commonly one per ordered block, requiring an ordered underlying organization. A clustered organization groups data physically by the index ordering; unclustered references can fetch qualifying records from many scattered pages. A table’s primary key and its physical clustering need not be identical.
Cost includes index traversal and data-page reads, considering cache state and selectivity. An index is not always best when a query returns most rows. GATE questions need key/pointer widths, records per block, clustering and root-caching assumptions before I/O estimates. An “index lookup is logarithmic” fact alone does not count all returned-record retrieval work.
''','A sorted file has block first keys 10,30,50. Sparse index is searched for key 37.',r'Find predecessor index key::::30 is the largest block-first key not exceeding 37.~Locate block::::Read the referenced block and search within it.~Interpret::::The index entry identifies a candidate block, not necessarily a direct record pointer for 37.', 'Search the data block beginning at key 30.','Sparse index correctness depends on the file’s sorted organization and its stated block boundaries.')
T('8-storage-index','trees','B trees;B+ trees','B/B+ trees, occupancy and range scans',r'''
A B-tree is a balanced multiway search tree whose nodes fit storage pages and hold multiple keys/children. A B+ tree keeps record entries at leaves while internal separator keys route searches; leaves are commonly linked for ordered scans. Definitions of “order” vary between maximum children and maximum keys, so use the problem’s convention.
An internal node with f children normally needs f pointers and f−1 separator keys; a leaf has a different layout including record pointers and a next-leaf link. Calculate capacities separately after reserved overhead. Occupancy bounds and root exceptions affect minimum/maximum heights. Insertion splits full nodes and can propagate upward; deletion can borrow or merge under the supplied minimum rules.
All leaves remain at the same depth. Range retrieval descends once to the first qualifying leaf then follows leaf links, instead of restarting from the root for every key. Count actual page accesses and possible data fetches separately. Matching tree height alone does not prove identical range costs between clustered and unclustered layouts.
''','Internal page size 100 bytes; pointer 4 bytes; key 8 bytes; no reserved overhead. Find max fanout.',r'Layout inequality::4f+8(f-1)\le100::f pointers and f−1 separator keys occupy the page.~Solve::12f\le108\Rightarrow f\le9::Fanout must be an integer.~Verify::4(9)+8(8)=100::Nine children fit exactly.', 'Maximum fanout 9.','Using nine keys as well as nine pointers would use a different and incorrect internal layout.')
T('8-transactions','acid','Transactions;ACID','Transactions and the ACID guarantees',r'''
A transaction groups operations into one logical unit. Atomicity makes its effects all succeed or all roll back; consistency preserves declared database invariants when transaction logic and enforcement are correct; isolation controls what concurrent executions can observe; durability retains committed effects after failures under the system’s guarantee.
These are different properties. A serial transaction can still violate an invariant if its logic is wrong, and durability does not prevent dirty reads. Logs and recovery mechanisms support atomicity/durability, while locks or other concurrency methods support isolation. Isolation levels may allow some anomalies, so do not equate every database’s default setting with full serializability.
A schedule interleaves transaction actions while preserving each transaction’s local order. Commit and abort mark outcomes; reading another transaction’s uncommitted write creates recovery risks. In GATE, identify which guarantee a failure scenario violates rather than choosing ACID as one undifferentiated label. An attempted operation and its durable committed result have distinct points in time.
''','A transfer subtracts 100 from account A, then crashes before adding it to B.',r'Partial effects::::Without recovery, total balance drops even though the intended transfer did not complete.~Atomicity requirement::::Both debit and credit must be treated as one unit, completed or undone.~Recovery outcome::::An uncommitted partial transfer should be rolled back under the stated atomicity mechanism.', 'Atomicity addresses the partial-transaction failure.','Durability instead concerns retaining a transfer that had already successfully committed.')
T('8-transactions','schedules','Serializability','Conflict graphs, recoverability and commit order',r'''
Conflicting actions belong to different transactions, access the same item and contain at least one write. Their order constrains any conflict-equivalent serial schedule. Create an edge Ti→Tj when Ti’s earlier conflicting action precedes Tj’s. The schedule is conflict serializable exactly when this directed graph is acyclic; a topological order supplies a serial witness.
Read/read pairs do not conflict, and actions on different items do not add edges. A cycle refutes conflict serializability, not every possible view-serializability notion. Blind writes can distinguish the two classes. Keep the transaction identities attached to each operation while scanning pairs.
Recovery tracks read-from dependencies and commit order, a separate question. If Tj reads Ti’s write, recoverability requires Ti commit before Tj commits. Cascadelessness forbids reading uncommitted writes; strictness also forbids overwriting them. A graph that is acyclic can still describe a recovery-unsafe execution if commits are ordered badly. Include commits when assessing those properties.
''','Schedule W1(X),R2(X),W2(Y),R1(Y).',r'Conflict on X::::W1 before R2 imposes T1→T2.~Conflict on Y::::W2 before R1 imposes T2→T1.~Cycle::::The two edges form a directed cycle, so no serial order respects both.', 'Not conflict serializable.','This conclusion does not need commit times, while recoverability would require read-from and commit/abort information.')
T('8-transactions','locking','Concurrency control;Two phase locking','Two-phase locking and strictness',r'''
Concurrency control coordinates simultaneous transactions to prevent disallowed anomalies. Two-phase locking divides a transaction into a growing phase with acquisitions and a shrinking phase with releases; after its first release it cannot acquire any new lock. Shared locks permit compatible reads, while exclusive locks protect conflicting writes under the lock table’s compatibility rules.
Basic 2PL guarantees conflict serializability but can deadlock and can permit recovery problems depending on release times. Strict 2PL holds exclusive locks until commit/abort, preventing other transactions from reading or overwriting those uncommitted protected writes. Holding all locks to completion is often called rigorous 2PL, so follow the problem’s terminology.
A transaction waiting for a lock must not proceed as though acquisition succeeded. Upgrades count as acquisitions and must respect the protocol. Alternative methods such as timestamp ordering or optimistic validation have different conflict handling. In GATE traces, test both phase ordering and compatibility; a serializable particular schedule need not mean every listed protocol rule was followed.
''','T1 acquires X, releases X, then requests Y. Does it obey basic 2PL?',r'Growing phase::::The first acquisition is permitted.~Begin shrinking::::Releasing X ends the period in which new acquisitions are allowed.~Later request::::Acquiring Y afterward violates the two-phase rule.', 'It violates 2PL.','The prohibition is about phase order, even if no other transaction currently holds Y.')
